   "title": "Korea's economy grows 1% in Q3 on chip exports, beating forecasts",
   "url": "https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/economy/20261027/koreas-economy-grows-1-in-q3-on-chip-exports-beating-forecasts",
   "domain": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "source": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "published": "Tue, 27 Oct 2026 02:22:03 GMT",
   "_dt": 1793067723.0,
   "ageDays": 0.02,
   "summary": "Korea's economy expanded 1 percent in the third quarter from the previous quarter, extending its stronger-than-expected recovery on the back of booming semiconductor exports, the Bank of Korea (BOK) said Tuesday. Exports grew 2.3 percent, driven mainly by semiconductors and machinery and electrical equipment. The stronger-than-expected growth puts the economy on a firmer footing to meet the BOK's growth forecast of 3.3 percent for this year, released in August.",
   "summary_zh": "韩国银行（BOK）周二表示，韩国经济第三季度较上一季度增长1%，在半导体出口强劲增长的推动下，延续了超出预期的复苏。出口增长2.3%，主要由半导体、机械和电气设备带动。高于预期的增长使经济基础更加稳固，有望实现韩国银行8月发布的今年经济增长3.3%的预测。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Korea's economy expanded 1 percent in the third quarter from the previous quarter, extending its stronger-than-expected recovery on the back of booming semiconductor exports, the Bank of Korea (BOK) said Tuesday. Preliminary data from the central bank showed that real gross domestic product (GDP) rose 1 percent in the July-September period, exceeding the BOK's own projection of 0.3 percent for the quarter. Facilities investment rose 4.1 percent, mainly on increased investment in machinery, including semiconductor manufacturing equipment. Investment in intellectual property products grew 2.3 percent as spending on research and development and software increased. Exports grew 2.3 percent, driven mainly by semiconductors and machinery and electrical equipment. Imports rose 2.4 percent, led by increases in crude oil and semiconductor manufacturing equipment. The stronger-than-expected growth puts the economy on a firmer footing to meet the BOK's growth forecast of 3.3 percent for this year, released in August. The country's quarterly growth rebounded sharply from a 0.1 percent contraction in the fourth quarter of 2025 to 1.8 percent in the first quarter, before expanding 0.6 percent in the second quarter, also beating expectations",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
