   "ageDays": 2.8,
   "summary": "Three tankers carrying Russian diesel are on their way to North America, Russian Deputy Prime Minister Alexander Novak said on Friday. \"The first three fuel tankers are already on their way to the eastern coast of the Atlantic Ocean, with significant support from the RDIF [Russian Direct Investment Fund],\" Novak said. A Reuters exclusive story published earlier on Friday, quoting ship-tracking data, said the first Russian diesel was on board a tanker headed for New York.",
   "summary_zh": "俄罗斯第一副总理亚历山大·诺瓦克周五表示，3艘装载俄罗斯柴油的油轮正在驶往北美。诺瓦克说：“首批3艘燃料油轮已经在驶往大西洋东海岸的途中，并得到了俄罗斯直接投资基金（RDIF）的大力支持。”路透社周五早些时候发布独家报道，援引船舶追踪数据称，首批俄罗斯柴油已经装上一艘驶往纽约的油轮。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Three tankers carrying Russian diesel are on their way to North America, Russian Deputy Prime Minister Alexander Novak said on Friday. Novak, in a statement quoted by Russian news agencies, said the cargos were part of a deal to supply U.S. and world markets. \"The first three fuel tankers are already on their way to the eastern coast of the Atlantic Ocean, with significant support from the RDIF [Russian Direct Investment Fund],\" Novak said. Novak said Russian companies could supply 500,000 metric tons in October. Under a deal announced this month by U.S. President Donald Trump, Russia agreed to supply diesel to global markets in an effort to lower fuel prices. The Trump administration temporarily lifted U.S. sanctions on Russian fuel that were designed to deprive Moscow of revenues for its war on Ukraine. The deal as announced by Trump provided for 300,000 metric tons in October and more later. A Reuters exclusive story published earlier on Friday, quoting ship-tracking data, said the first Russian diesel was on board a tanker headed for New York. We are facing unprecedented challenges. Russia's Prosecutor General's Office has designated The Moscow Times as an \"undesirable\" organization, criminalizing our work and putting our staff at risk of prosecution. This follows our earlier unjust labeling as a \"foreign agent.\" These actions are direct attempts to silence independent journalism in Russia. The authorities claim our work \"discredits the decisions of the Russian leadership.\" We see things differently: we strive to provide accurate, unbiased reporting on Russia. We, the journalists of The Moscow Times, refuse to be silenced. But to continue our work, we need your help . Your support, no matter how small, makes a world of difference. If you can, please support us monthly starting from just $ 2. It's quick to set up, and every contribution makes a significant impact. By supporting The Moscow Times, you're defending open, independent journalism in the face of repression. Thank you for standing with us",
